 on Religious Education","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Governance Committee","time":"2.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"West Sussex Fire & Rescue Service Local Pension Board","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Health and Adult Social Care Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-18","committee":"Performance and Finance Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"West Sussex Council's finances worsen as children's and adult care costs climb","lead":"West Sussex County Council faces worsening financial pressures in its budget for the year to March 2027, Cabinet members heard today. According to the council's corporate risk assessment, its financial sustainability risk score has risen to 25, against a target of 12 and up from 16 last year, signalling deteriorating financial headroom. The council attributes the pressure to lack of new funding from central government, combined with inflation and rising interest rates. Children's and adult services together are projecting a \u00a325m overspend against an approved budget of \u00a3885m. The council is managing the pressure through planned withdrawals from reserves, money the council has set aside, expected to fall by \u00a320m over the same period. The council has also deferred \u00a314.2m of planned capital spending, money for buildings, vehicles and infrastructure, reducing its capital programme from \u00a3142.3m to \u00a3128.1m.","iso":"2026-09-22","paper_title":"Appendix 5 - Corporate Risk Register Summary","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61979/61979.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"West Sussex braces for \u00a324.5m overspend as children's and adult care costs surge","lead":"West Sussex County Council is forecasting a year-end overspend of \u00a324.5 million, driven by soaring costs in its two largest demand-led services. Children, Young People and Learning accounts for \u00a316.1 million of the shortfall, and Adults Services for \u00a39 million. The council's own financial monitoring report attributes the pressures to rising demand and costs rather than management failure. The overspend emerges alongside a sharp deterioration in the council's financial risk rating, which has climbed to 25 against a target of 12, citing lack of central government funding, inflation and interest rate rises, and growing pressures in social care, home-to-school transport and support for children with special educational needs. The Performance and Finance Scrutiny Committee received these figures at its 18 September meeting.","iso":"2026-09-18","paper_title":"Item_5_Appendix_5_Corporate_Risk_Register_Summary","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62006/62006.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"West Sussex forecasts \u00a324.5m budget overspend as children's and adults services face demand surge","lead":"West Sussex Council expects to overspend its budget by \u00a324.5m by year end, driven primarily by demand-led services struggling to contain costs. Children, Young People and Learning alone faces a \u00a316m overspend, and Adults Services a further \u00a39m. The council's own risk assessment shows financial sustainability has deteriorated sharply, with its risk score rising to 25 against a target of 12, citing lack of new central government funding combined with inflation, rising interest rates and growing pressures in social care, home-to-school transport and support for children with special educational needs. The mid-year position suggests these cost pressures will persist unless brought under control in the remainder of the financial year.","iso":"2026-09-18","paper_title":"Item_5_Appendix_5_Corporate_Risk_Register_Summary","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s62006/62006.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","headline":"High Needs Stability Grant (\u00a3182.7m) pending DfE approval; would materially ease DSG and treasury pressures if approved","detail":"The government announced High Needs Stability Grants to councils with DSG deficits, covering up to 90% of accumulated High Needs DSG deficit through end of 2025/26. West Sussex has applied for approximately \u00a3182.7m, contingent on DfE approval of its Local SEND Reform Plan (submitted June 2026). DfE is expected to communicate assessment outcomes in September 2026. If approved, this would significantly reduce DSG-related cashflow pressure and free up cash for investment.","category":"income","matters":"If approved by DfE in September, would materially improve council's treasury position and cash availability, materially offsetting much of the DSG deficit impact.","figures":"\u00a3182.7m grant application, 90% of High Needs deficit eligible, DfE decision expected September 2026","quote":"For West Sussex, this could equate to approximately \u00a3182.7m. However, payment is contingent upon the Council's Local SEND Reform Plan being approved by the Department for Education (DfE), which was submitted in June 2026.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","headline":"Fairer Funding Reforms will reduce council funding by estimated \u00a330m over the next three years","detail":"Beyond current pressures, the council faces an estimated reduction in funding of \u00a330m over the next three years from Fairer Funding Reforms. This is described as a major funding challenge that will have material impact on the council's medium-term sustainability and resilience. This structural funding decline compounds current-year overspend pressures.","category":"income","matters":"Creates structural medium-term funding deficit; worsens financial sustainability outlook and reduces flexibility to address current pressures.","figures":"\u00a330m reduction over three years from Fairer Funding Reforms","quote":"Fairer Funding Reforms \u2013 An estimated reduction in funding of \u00a330m over the next three years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","headline":"Doubtful Debt Provision risk of up to \u00a36m additional requirement, adding to overspend pressure","detail":"Doubtful Debt Provision has increased to \u00a317.7m (31 March 2026). Based on ongoing work, there is significant risk of further increase of up to \u00a36m in 2026/27, creating additional budget pressure. Council is securing additional resources for debt recovery and review of outstanding debts. Potential mitigations include higher investment income and lower borrowing costs, or use of Contingency Budget.","category":"income","matters":"Bad debt risk; if materialised, adds up to \u00a36m to the overspend, worsening financial position.","figures":"\u00a317.7m Doubtful Debt Provision, up to \u00a36m additional risk in 2026/27","quote":"Based on the work completed to date, there is a significant risk that the required provision will increase, resulting in an additional funding requirement of up to \u00a36m in 2026/27.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","headline":"Investment income forecast to fall \u00a39.3m in 2026/27 as DSG deficit requirements consume cash balances","detail":"The council must fund the DSG deficit from its own cash balances, reducing funds available for investment. This creates a forecast loss of \u00a39.3m of investment income in 2026/27, compared to \u00a36.9m loss in prior year. The investment budget assumes 3.42% average return. This loss would be significantly mitigated if the High Needs Stability Grant (\u00a3182.7m) is approved, as more cash would become available for investment.","category":"income","matters":"Compounds the \u00a324.5m overspend by reducing offsetting investment returns; worsens if the DSG grant is not approved.","figures":"\u00a39.3m loss 2026/27, \u00a36.9m loss 2025/26, 3.42% assumed average return","quote":"The requirement for the Council to fund the Dedicated Schools Grant (DSG) deficit from its own cash balances continues to reduce the level of funds available for investment, resulting in a forecast loss of approximately \u00a39.3m of investment income in 2026/27 (\u00a36.9m loss in 2025/26).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-22","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","headline":"Fourth consecutive year of reserve drawdowns planned; council acknowledges this pattern is unsustainable","detail":"To balance the forecast \u00a324.5m overspend, the council plans to draw on reserves (Contingency Budget or Budget Management Reserve). This would be the fourth consecutive year of accessing reserves as one-off funding. The council explicitly states this pattern is not sustainable. General Fund Reserve stands at \u00a336.2m (30 June 2026), described as the ultimate financial safeguard to be used only as a last resort.","category":"reserves","matters":"Indicates structural deficit rather than one-off pressures; demonstrates declining financial resilience and sustainability, a key concern for lenders.","figures":"\u00a336.2m General Fund Reserve, \u00a310m Contingency Budget, fourth consecutive year of reserve drawdown","quote":"This would be the fourth consecutive year in a row this one-off funding was accessed, and this continued use of reserves is not sustainable.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-22"},"name":"West Sussex CC","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-23","since_iso":"2026-08-24","distress":false,"headline":"High Needs Stability Grant (\u00a3182.7m) pending DfE approval; would materially ease DSG and treasury pressures if approved","lead":"If approved by DfE in September, would materially improve council's treasury position and cash availability, materially offsetting much of the DSG deficit impact.","also":["54 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Fire & Rescue Service Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-25","n_findings":55,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Annex B - Performance and Resources Report - Q1 2026/27","top_paper_url":"https://westsussex.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61965/61965.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":197,"news_drafts_total":3}}